---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import LoopSlider from "../components/LoopSlider.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// No hero photograph. The template's artwork is a placeholder and the obvious
// replacement — a stock lecture theatre, or something generated — would say
// nothing this course is about. The arithmetic below is the argument, so it
// gets the space instead.
//
// 130 hours is one 6-unit course. Attempts = 130h / (time to failure + time to
// retry), rounded to something honest rather than something precise.
// Failure and retry are one column, not two. They are a pair — the loop is
// their sum — and splitting them cost 130px of table width, which at 390px
// pushed the attempts column, the only one the table exists to deliver, off
// the right of the screen behind a scrollbar nobody is told about.
const loops = [
  { what: "A Souls boss", loop: "90 s → 15 s", attempts: "~4,500" },
  { what: "A ranked match", loop: "30 min → 1 min", attempts: "~250" },
  { what: "A weekly quiz", loop: "1 week → 1 week", attempts: "12" },
  { what: "An assignment", loop: "3 weeks → never", attempts: "3" },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>Why one of these is easy and the other is not</h2>

  <p>
    Nobody needs discipline to put 130 hours into League of Legends. You do not
    plan it, you do not force yourself, you queue again. Put the same 130 hours
    into a course and it is grim: you sit down, you struggle, and most evenings
    you cannot say what moved.
  </p>

  <p>
    Same hours. Same person. <strong>What differs is how fast the work pays you
    back.</strong> A game tells you constantly whether you are getting anywhere.
    A course tells you in three weeks, in red pen.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the whole course is one practical question: can you build that reward for
    yourself, and finish a course the way you finish a game &mdash; one step at
    a time, absorbed, and without it costing you everything you have?
  </p>

  <p>
    Two kinds of game answer two different halves of it, and they are not
    interchangeable.
  </p>

  <h2>Part one &mdash; making every minute worth using</h2>

  <p>
    You sit down for half an hour. Or an hour, or a whole morning, or a whole
    day. It is painful, you cannot get into it, and at the end of it almost
    nothing has advanced. Everyone knows this state and nobody schedules it.
  </p>

  <p>
    A MOBA never once lets it happen. Minions arrive every thirty seconds and
    each one is worth gold if you take it cleanly. Leaving your lane costs a
    known amount and might buy a kill somewhere else. Dragon and herald spawn on
    a clock the whole map can see. <strong>There is never a minute in which the
    right thing to do is nothing</strong>, and you always know what this minute
    is for.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is what weeks 2&ndash;5 are for. Not to admire the design &mdash; to
    take it apart and build the same thing over your own thirty minutes, so that
    every minute of a sitting is a minute you can use.
  </p>

  <h2>Part two &mdash; how to start something far too hard</h2>

  <p>
    A Souls game is not a forty-minute match. It is a wall of bosses, and the
    first time you meet one you die in a minute or two.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the part everyone reads as cruelty and it is the opposite. Dying in
    ninety seconds is short enough that you come away holding <strong>exactly
    one thing</strong> &mdash; <em>it always sweeps after the second swing</em>
    &mdash; so the next attempt has exactly one new goal, one thing to fix, one
    question to answer. You are not being brave. You are curious whether that
    one change is enough.
  </p>

  <p>
    Twenty attempts later the boss is dead and <strong>you never planned
    anything</strong>. The impossible task broke itself into small ones, a
    lesson at a time, because the loop was short enough to only carry one.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is weeks 6&ndash;9: how to get started on something too hard to start,
    and how to make a thing that big become a thing that small. No ladder gives
    you this, however well you build it.
  </p>

  <h2>Part three &mdash; the run</h2>

  <p>
    Weeks 10&ndash;12 you point both at the course you stalled on &mdash; the
    one you started, stopped, restarted and stopped again, and still believe you
    need. You cover a quarter of it in about thirty hours and sit an exam we set
    on the part you chose. Everything else you hand in is something you wrote
    about yourself. That one isn&rsquo;t.
  </p>

  <h2>The same claim, in numbers</h2>

  <p>
    All of it rests on one measurable thing: how long you work before you find
    out whether it worked. Over a fixed 130 hours, that interval decides how
    many chances you get.
  </p>

  <div class="loop-table-wrap">
    <table class="loop-table">
      <caption>
        Correction opportunities across one 130-hour course. The first two are
        130&nbsp;h divided by the loop; the last two are simply how many the
        course gives you &mdash; twelve teaching weeks, three assessments.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Loop</th>
          <th scope="col">Failure &rarr; retry</th>
          <th scope="col">Attempts in 130&nbsp;h</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          loops.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.what}</th>
              <td class="num">{row.loop}</td>
              <td class="num attempts">{row.attempts}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="pull">
    A Souls boss gives you fifteen hundred times as many as an assignment does.
    That is the entire reason one of them is easy to spend a hundred hours on.
  </p>

  <LoopSlider />

  <p>
    Everything from week 2 onwards is about moving yourself leftward along this.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>

  <p>
    Anyone who has watched a semester go past without being able to say what
    they did with it, and anyone carrying a subject they keep starting over.
    There are no prerequisites and the reading is light, but this is not a soft
    course: you will play two demanding games attentively, ship a mechanism
    that survives you being tired, and then go back to something you have
    already failed to get through.
  </p>

  <p>
    You do not need to write code. Two of the three assessments offer a
    paper route and a built route, marked identically, and a paper system in
    daily use beats an unfinished program every time.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, and the argument each one is responsible for.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>
        Two of them, in weeks 3 and 7, and both are spent playing. That is not
        a joke.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>
        Two live demonstrations, and one run at the wall you already failed to
        get over.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
      <p>
        Including why a different exam for every student is marked the way it
        is.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* The scroller is a fallback, not the plan. At 390px the wrapper is 354
     wide and the table wants 540, which put the fourth column — the one the
     whole table exists to deliver — off-screen behind a scroll nobody is
     told about. So the table is allowed to shrink instead (below), and this
     only catches whatever is left. */
  .loop-table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-block: 1.5rem;
  }

  .loop-table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 24rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  @media (width <= 34rem) {
    .loop-table {
      min-width: 0;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
    }

    .loop-table th,
    .loop-table td {
      padding: 0.5rem 0.4rem;
    }

    .loop-table th:first-child,
    .loop-table td:first-child {
      padding-left: 0;
    }

    .loop-table th:last-child,
    .loop-table td:last-child {
      padding-right: 0;
    }

    /* Let the two-word column headings stack rather than force the table
       wider than the screen to keep them on one line. */
    .loop-table thead th {
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    }
  }

  .loop-table caption {
    caption-side: bottom;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--at-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .loop-table th,
  .loop-table td {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* `--at-table-header-text`, not `--at-text-secondary`. The theme fills the
     header row with the brand gold, and secondary text is mixed for the page
     background, so pairing the two gave 2.42:1 — and it was invisible to me
     twice, because both of my first two contrast probes resolved the header's
     background as the page's instead of compositing the ancestor chain. */
  .loop-table thead th {
    color: var(--at-table-header-text);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  .loop-table tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .loop-table .num {
    font-family: var(--at-font-mono);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* The rightmost column is the punchline, so it is the only thing on the
     table wearing the brand colour.

     NOT `var(--at-primary)` straight, which is the lockup gold and lands at
     3.44:1 on the light surface — under the 4.5:1 AA floor for text this size,
     and 18px bold is just short of the 18.66px that would let it use the 3:1
     large-text floor instead. axe passes it either way: it cannot resolve
     `oklch()` inside `light-dark()`, so it reports the pair as incomplete
     rather than failing. Measured, not assumed. Darkened for the light scheme
     and lifted for the dark one, both derived from the brand hue so a brand
     change still carries. */
  .loop-table .attempts {
    color: light-dark(
      oklch(from var(--at-primary) 42% c h),
      oklch(from var(--at-primary) 84% c h)
    );
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  /* The rule carries the brand colour here and the words do not: a 3px border
     is decoration, so it is not held to a text-contrast floor, and the pull
     quote reads at full strength instead of at 3.44:1. */
  .pull {
    margin-block: 1.5rem 2.5rem;
    padding-left: 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
    color: var(--at-text);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>
